      an external doc or silently violate the convention.
---

## Context

This submodule implements both delivery channels the parent spec requires.
The handshake channel matters because it is the only documentation surface
a compliant MCP client is required to present to the model verbatim, in
every session, in every project, without user action. The install channel
matters because a one-command bootstrap is the only onboarding path that
does not depend on the installing agent remembering or paraphrasing what
AIDE is — and that one command is reachable two ways: the `aide_init` MCP
tool when the user is talking to an agent connected to this server, and the
npx CLI when the user has nothing connected yet. Both routes invoke the
same install service; neither route teaches AIDE doctrine. Between the
handshake (teaching) and the install action (artifact delivery), the
parent's "no paraphrasing, no per-project drift" guarantee lives or dies
here.

Everything else — how many tools, how descriptions are written, how the
install action handles idempotency — is in service of keeping those two
channels faithful to the canonical docs above.

## Strategy

**The tool list is the primary teaching artifact for channel one, not an
API reference.** Each description introduces the concept it invokes before
invoking it: `aide_discover` explains what `.aide` files are and why you
read them before code; `aide_scaffold` explains the naming-conflict rule
before it describes its `type` enum. An agent that only ever reads the
tool list must still come away with the full convention. The cost of a
longer description is recovered every time an agent doesn't need to fetch
an external doc, and dwarfed by the cost of an agent silently violating
the convention.

**The teaching weight across tool descriptions is uneven, and that is
deliberate.** `aide_discover` and `aide_scaffold` carry most of the
load — they introduce the file types, the placement and naming rules,
the read-before-code workflow. The remaining tool descriptions
(`aide_read`, `aide_validate`, `aide_upgrade`, `aide_info`,
`aide_inspect`, and the `aide_init` description itself) are mostly
operational, naming what the tool does without re-teaching the
methodology behind it. This is acceptable because the handshake is not
the only teaching surface. The install action installs a `CLAUDE.md`
pointer stub that the host agent reads at session-start on every
session, AIDE-related or not, and that stub names the on-disk doc hub
the agent crawls when a task actually touches `.aide` material. Tool
descriptions reinforce the doctrine when an agent invokes a specific
tool; the always-on session-start teaching comes from the stub-plus-hub
that channel two installs. A tool description that is light on
methodology is fine when the channel-two installation is doing its job.

**Tool descriptions and host-installed methodology artifacts are composed
from docs/, never hand-written.** The canonical doc files under docs/ are
read at build time (for tool description text) and at install time (for
both the pointer stub the install service writes into the host's config
file and the doc-hub files it lands on the host's disk). No AIDE doctrine
lives as a string literal in this submodule's source — doctrine lives in
docs/, and this submodule is a renderer. This is how the parent's
single-source-of-truth invariant survives contact with the server's
runtime.

**Delivery to the host practices progressive disclosure at the file
layer, not just at the doc layer.** The install action does not dump the
full methodology into the host's config file. It writes a short pointer
stub into the config file and lands the full methodology doc set into a
dedicated doc hub on the host's disk. The config file — which the agent
reads on every session — carries only the knowledge that AIDE exists,
where the hub lives, and when to crawl into it. The hub itself is read
only by sessions whose current task actually touches `.aide` files. The
pattern mirrors how `study-playbook` works against the user's global
brain: a shallow pointer surface that enumerates a deep content surface,
with the agent crawling on demand instead of carrying everything on
every read.

**Each tool covers a distinct lifecycle phase with no overlap.** Discover
is the map; read is the drill; scaffold is the pen; validate is the health
check; init is the bootstrap; upgrade is the updater. Adding a new tool
requires showing that no existing tool can absorb the responsibility
without overlap. A tighter surface means less for an agent to get wrong
and less description budget competing for attention at handshake time.

**Client parity via MCP, not per-client shims.** The server speaks
standard MCP over stdio. All client-specific branching lives in the
install service, which writes config files for whichever framework it
detects. The other tools are fed identical input schemas regardless of
which client is talking, because parity is what lets the teaching
surface be reused across clients.

**Two entry points, one install service.** The `aide_init` MCP tool and
the npx CLI are entry points into the same install service that lives
under the install module. They diverge only in their wire shape — the
MCP tool exchanges structured JSON with a calling agent and pauses for
inherent decisions through `AskUserQuestion`; the CLI runs end-to-end
in a terminal session and pauses for inherent decisions through stdin
prompts. The install service itself is consumer-agnostic: same plan
shape, same status enum, same idempotency contract, same prescriptions
for MCP config merge. Adding a new entry point is permissible if and
only if the new entry point installs (it does not teach AIDE doctrine);
a CLI that walks an agent through the methodology interactively is the
forbidden third channel, not a thin invocation of channel two.

**Idempotency as an invariant, not a feature.** The install action,
scaffold, and validate are all safe to re-run on a project already in
that state. Agents retry tool calls when uncertain; CLI users re-run
commands when uncertain; a non-idempotent call corrupts on the retry.
This is especially load-bearing for the install action, which writes
files neither an agent nor a developer should feel nervous about
re-running.

## Good examples

A new agent connects to aidemd-mcp for the first time and, before
touching a single file, reasons:

> There are .aide spec files next to orchestrator code that I should
> read before I read the code. The canonical file types are: `.aide`,
> the default intent spec; `intent.aide`, the same thing renamed when
> a `research.aide` sits alongside; `research.aide`, raw research;
> `plan.aide`, the architect's checkboxed implementation plan;
> `todo.aide`, a QA re-alignment document. I must never see both `.aide` and
> `intent.aide` in the same folder. My workflow is: call `aide_discover`
> with no path for the project map, then drill into the relevant subtree
> with a path, then `aide_read` specific specs as needed.

Every piece of that reasoning came from the tool list alone. No
CLAUDE.md was read; no README was fetched. That is the target experience
at handshake time.

A user invokes the install action on a fresh project — either by asking
the agent to call `aide_init`, or by running `npx aidemd-mcp init` in a
terminal — and gets back a summary like:

> Detected framework: Claude Code.
> Created CLAUDE.md with AIDE pointer stub naming the doc hub at .aide/.
> Installed .aide/ doc hub with the canonical methodology docs and a hub index.
> Created slash commands for the pipeline phases.
> Wired aidemd-mcp into .mcp.json.
> Configured .zed/settings.json to render .aide as Markdown.
> Skipped VS Code extension — `code` CLI not on PATH.

Running the same install action a second time, through either entry
point, returns the same summary with "already present" markers instead
of "created", and nothing on disk changes. The install service is what
guarantees this; the entry point is just a wire shape.

A maintainer edits `docs/aide-spec.md` to clarify the placement rule.
They rebuild the server; the next agent to connect sees the updated text
in the `aide_discover` tool description because the description template
reads from the doc at build time. They also run the install action on a
host project; the updated text lands in the host's `.aide/aide-spec.md`
because the doc-hub installer reads the same canonical source. One edit
propagated through both channels — handshake teaching and installed
host-side content — with no duplication in between.

An agent in a host project opens a file unrelated to AIDE to fix a bug.
It reads CLAUDE.md at session start, sees the short pointer stub, notes
that the `.aide/` doc hub exists, and consciously skips crawling it
because the current task touches no `.aide` files. Total AIDE cost paid
by that session: a handful of lines of stub. Later the same agent picks
up a task that does touch `.aide` files, follows the stub's pointer into
`.aide/`, reads the hub index, and drills into `aide-spec.md` and
`aide-template.md` — and only those. The delivery layer practiced the
same progressive disclosure the methodology teaches.

## Bad examples

A tool description that reads "Scaffold new .aide files. See the AIDE
docs for naming rules." The agent has learned nothing about the
naming-conflict rule and will happily create a `.aide` next to an
existing `research.aide`, producing exactly the corruption the tool was
built to prevent. The description failed its only job.

A tool description hand-written as a template literal in
`src/tools/scaffold/index.ts`, containing AIDE rules the maintainer
typed from memory. The canonical doc under docs/ has since been updated;
the literal has not; the two now disagree, and the server's handshake
ships a different doctrine than the install action does. The
single-source-of-truth invariant has been broken at the submodule level.

An install action that appends its pointer stub to CLAUDE.md on every
run, so a project initialized three times — through any combination of
entry points — has three stacked marker-bounded regions. Idempotency
was treated as a nice-to-have instead of an invariant living at the
service layer, and the file is now corrupted.

An install action that skips the doc-hub step and dumps the full
methodology body inside CLAUDE.md's stub region instead. Every non-
AIDE session in the host now re-reads several hundred lines of
doctrine on every open, and the refactor that this shape was meant
to enforce has been quietly reversed at the delivery layer while
passing every test.

A tool — say `aide_lint` — added to the surface because validation rules
"felt different" from health checks. Both tools show up in the handshake,
overlap in responsibility, and the agent has to guess which one to call.
The surface has been diluted for no gain, because the responsibility should
have lived inside validate.
